  "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-3,-1,4)$ and $Q(1,8,9).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, starting with the equation of the line using the cross product, we manipulate the equation and then substitute in our values of $p$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v \\\\ r\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\ r\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =-41i-31j+23k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
